    "solution": "Cross the submerged reef and swim under the sluice stone. Dash upward to park it against its stop. Swim east to the tall floating crate and climb its left face while the tide is high. Jump right, then dash horizontally onto the high gallery. Cut the hinged partition into a bridge. Walk over it and drop between its end and the far column; continue below the locked divider into the lower-right anvil basin. Stop on the broad anvil, make a small hop, and cut down. Its rebound reaches the eight-tile one-way tally balcony, 96 pixels above the anvil; an ordinary jump and updash fall short. The tally opens the divider and the west exit. Hop left over the short tooth-lined gap, climb the newly accessible face of the return column, and cross the turned partition back to the gallery. Wait for the crate to rise again, hop down onto it, and use a left/up dash from its left edge to return to the raised sluice stone. Rest there, then make the small final hop into the sheltered western exit gallery.",
    "nearestExisting": "B13 manufactures a stopper for a horizontal block; B12 trades a beam shield for a bridge; the original Landing Shores rooms use tidal floats for launch timing. This room uses submerged thorns to permit access below a full-width sluice stone. Its upward displacement simultaneously opens the wet outward route and leaves a permanent dry stair for a return directly above that route. No lantern transport or optical circuit is involved.",
    "novelty": "The same float now has two necessary traversal roles across a wide basin: high-tide access to the first gallery and a high return takeoff after the lower sluice. The raised stone is a permanent landing above the reef, but is too far from the eastern gallery to replace the float. The lower-right route passes under a divider whose far tally exposes the return column.",
