    "interconnection": "The outbound passage runs underneath a permanently raised stone, then the tidal crate links it to the high gallery. The turned hinge is crossed out and back. Entering beneath the far divider leads to a physically necessary anvil launch; the tally exposes the return column. The same crate and raised stone then connect the upper route home.",
    "superroom": {
      "screenAreas": 3.8260869565217392,
      "stages": [
        "Flooded reef, sluice stone and tide-borne high takeoff",
        "Turned partition, lower-right underpass and anvil balcony",
        "Opened return column, rising crate and permanent dry stair"
      ]
    },
    "entrance": "West low chamber; the first full tide already covers the thorn reef.",
    "exit": "West upper gallery, directly over the outbound tunnel.",
    "endingForgiveness": "The anvil and one-way tally balcony are eight tiles wide, with a safe floor below a missed bounce. A two-frame earlier downward cut passes. The return can be delayed a full second on the broad gallery; a three-second rest on the final four-tile stone also passes. The final hop rises only 16 pixels and leads into a long sheltered gallery.",
    "designNotes": "No event-only completion requirements were added. The raised-stone-to-gallery gap defeats a direct jump/dash; the gallery overhang has visible downward teeth, preventing a swim/updash/climb approach from replacing the float. The tally divider reaches the ceiling and has a marked lower tooth lip, so its tall return face is only usable after opening. The tally balcony sits 96 pixels above the broad anvil, beyond ordinary jump/updash height. Original successful float and anvil bypass tapes are preserved in review.reportedBypasses and replayed as negatives, alongside three active alternatives against the revised geometry. Tide starts high using the existing reversed endpoints; both high and low holds are 600 frames. All movement remains ordinary Base movement. A small sight glass beside the return gallery tracks the actual tide surface, so the lowered float has a visible cue while below the camera.",
